        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">COPD exacerbation, treated with bronchodilators and steroids</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your patient, a 67-year-old female admitted on 02/04/2008 due to an acute exacerbation of her known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, she presented with increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Chest X-ray confirmed pulmonary hyperinflation without pneumonia or consolidation. Arterial blood gas analysis revealed mild hypoxaemia with hypercapnia. Blood tests showed mild inflammatory markers. She was treated with nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, and supplemental oxygen therapy. Vital signs were monitored continuously throughout the stay. The patient responded well to treatment with significant symptomatic improvement. She was discharged in stable condition with appropriate inhaler therapy and a short course of oral prednisolone. I recommend close follow-up and review of her long-term COPD management. Please do not hesitate to contact me for further information.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
